        "bg_color": "#f5fbf6",
        "default_desc": "South Indian homes and restaurants can never complete their menu without the popular Vanjaram Meen Varuval. Of all fish fries, the Vanjaram — king fish — is widely considered the best. The secret lies in the spice combination and the patience of a good marinade.\n\nThick slices of firm seer fish are marinated in a fiery blend of roasted spices, ginger, and garlic, then pan-fried to crispy perfection. The addition of rice flour ensures a perfect crust while locking in the ocean-fresh moisture. Serve it hot alongside sambar, rasam, or simply with curd rice — a combination beloved along the Tamil Nadu coast.",
        "prep_time": "20 m",
        "cook_time": "5 m",
        "servings": "4 people",
        "author": "TruSea Chef",
        "calories": "250",
        "protein": "22g",
        "fat": "16g",
        "carbs": "4g",
        "cooks_notes_title": "Cook's Wisdom",
        "cooks_notes": "The fish does not turn fully crisp, but the shallow fry creates a partially smoked masala coat — that is the magic of this dish.\n\nMedium-low heat is non-negotiable. High heat scorches the spices before the fish is cooked through.\n\nTamarind pulp is recommended over lime for a richer, more rounded tang in the marinade.\n\nServe alongside steamed rice, sambar, or rasam. Curd rice makes a perfect pairing — a combination that has been a coastal favourite for generations."
      }
